  "material_units": [
    {
      "key": "primer-basics.html::node-475",
      "source": "SafeLearning/primer-basics.html",
      "locator": "::node-475",
      "line": 480,
      "tag": "div",
      "classes": [],
      "source_text": "The codomain is the declared target set. The image contains only outputs actually reached. Compare $f(-1)$ and $f(1)$.",
      "text_sha256": "3b7d3ddc210d41f90e86f627cadeb495ef7896d90df2f85810cac46d7dcb287f",
      "source_sha256": "7aded044a9c433031999bcd57319d4735782d32888d730068a079c005e5286b4",
      "inventory_kind": "surrounding_prose_needs_semantic_classification",
      "review_status": "approved_complete_source",
      "semantic_kind": "proved",
      "material_status": "proved",
      "lean_declarations": [
        "SafeLearning.CompleteFoundationsLogic.finite_square_image",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteFoundationsTeaching.finite_square_classification",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteFoundationsFunctionInverses.actual_injective_surjective_and_bijective_definitions"
      ],
      "hypotheses": [
        "Exact source finite integer domain {-2,-1,0,1,2} and declared target {0,1,4,9}."
      ],
      "missing_clauses": [],
      "per_unit_reason": "The true finite image contains precisely attained outputs, the actual typed target retains 9, and the genuine -1/1 collision establishes the requested comparison. Domain/codomain are exact function specification, not inferred from its formula."
    },
    {
      "key": "primer-basics.html::node-489",
      "source": "SafeLearning/primer-basics.html",
      "locator": "::node-489",
      "line": 500,
      "tag": "div",
      "classes": [],
      "source_text": "Apply the function on the right first: $(f\\circ g)(x)=f(g(x))$.",
      "text_sha256": "093c5c9e5bcfa61feced36b6ec755fe3dccb157820eaf25e709599870d84b441",
      "source_sha256": "7aded044a9c433031999bcd57319d4735782d32888d730068a079c005e5286b4",
      "inventory_kind": "surrounding_prose_needs_semantic_classification",
      "review_status": "approved_complete_source",
      "semantic_kind": "proved",
      "material_status": "proved",
      "lean_declarations": [
        "SafeLearning.CompleteFoundationsLogic.composition_examples"
      ],
      "hypotheses": [
        "Actual real functions f(x)=2x-1 and g(x)=x^2."
      ],
      "missing_clauses": [],
      "per_unit_reason": "Read actual Function.comp reduction and both full computed general formulas; rightmost function is applied first by the ordinary composition definition."
    },
    {
      "key": "primer-basics.html::node-495",
      "source": "SafeLearning/primer-basics.html",
      "locator": "::node-495",
      "line": 505,
      "tag": "p",
      "classes": [],
      "source_text": "The order of composition matters; the two functions are different.",
      "text_sha256": "805aa177ce96c251bba93cf7c7988a6eb4acf50b0e20b1ac1bc8e6da0da8a7ee",
      "source_sha256": "7aded044a9c433031999bcd57319d4735782d32888d730068a079c005e5286b4",
      "inventory_kind": "surrounding_prose_needs_semantic_classification",
      "review_status": "approved_complete_source",
      "semantic_kind": "proved",
      "material_status": "proved",
      "lean_declarations": [
        "SafeLearning.CompleteFoundationsLogic.composition_examples"
      ],
      "hypotheses": [
        "The two particular functions in exercise F2; no claim that every pair of functions fails to commute."
      ],
      "missing_clauses": [],
      "per_unit_reason": "The actual compositions give 7 and 9 at input2. Their unequal evaluations prove that these two particular functions differ; order matters in this source example."
    },
    {
      "key": "primer-basics.html::node-504",
      "source": "SafeLearning/primer-basics.html",
      "locator": "::node-504",
      "line": 520,
      "tag": "div",
      "classes": [],
      "source_text": "Multiply the fixed-point equation by $4$. For the iteration, feed each new output back into $g$.",
      "text_sha256": "1504c88cc66c356042ed6a5f3eebeca9925410752f6ce09dbb945a9358f91fea",
      "source_sha256": "7aded044a9c433031999bcd57319d4735782d32888d730068a079c005e5286b4",
      "inventory_kind": "surrounding_prose_needs_semantic_classification",
      "review_status": "approved_complete_source",
      "semantic_kind": "proved",
      "material_status": "proved",
      "lean_declarations": [
        "SafeLearning.PrimersFoundations.affine_fixed_point",
        "SafeLearning.PrimersFoundations.affine_iteration",
        "SafeLearning.PrimersFoundations.affine_error",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteFoundationsLogic.quarter_iteration_error"
      ],
      "hypotheses": [
        "Actual g(x)=(x+3)/4, nonzero denominator4, initial -1."
      ],
      "missing_clauses": [],
      "per_unit_reason": "The exact fixed-point equivalence uses the stated multiplication by4 and solves x=1. Genuine consecutive outputs and all-index iteration/error definitions justify feeding the new output back, without merely assuming a recurrence conclusion."
    },
    {
      "key": "primer-basics.html::node-519",
      "source": "SafeLearning/primer-basics.html",
      "locator": "::node-519",
      "line": 540,
      "tag": "div",
      "classes": [],
      "source_text": "For an image, collect squares of permitted inputs. For a preimage, solve the output condition for every possible real input, including negative inputs.",
      "text_sha256": "244020bc3983d719290d9c355fdedb4ce490ee090e1e9346328070f0ba76563a",
      "source_sha256": "7aded044a9c433031999bcd57319d4735782d32888d730068a079c005e5286b4",
      "inventory_kind": "surrounding_prose_needs_semantic_classification",
      "review_status": "approved_complete_source",
      "semantic_kind": "proved",
      "material_status": "proved",
      "lean_declarations": [
        "SafeLearning.CompleteFoundationsLogic.square_interval_image",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteFoundationsLogic.square_interval_preimage",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteFoundationsLogic.negative_square_preimage",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteFoundationsFunctionInverses.actual_preimages_exist_for_every_function_without_an_inverse"
      ],
      "hypotheses": [
        "Actual real square map and source intervals [-3,1] and [1,4], including both negative and positive possible inputs."
      ],
      "missing_clauses": [],
      "per_unit_reason": "The actual image proof collects squared permitted inputs and constructs every image witness using -sqrt y. The actual preimage proof divides by the sign of the input and keeps both negative and positive interval branches; negative-square preimage is empty without requiring an inverse function."
    },
    {
      "key": "primer-basics.html::node-540",
      "source": "SafeLearning/primer-basics.html",
      "locator": "::node-540",
      "line": 560,
      "tag": "div",
      "classes": [],
      "source_text": "Restricting inputs can remove repeated outputs. Restricting the declared target can remove unreachable outputs.",
      "text_sha256": "bf21762bf81e3550c5c315723ea10144a6ee3cadcb240e5a6102c444f1b5bd41",
      "source_sha256": "7aded044a9c433031999bcd57319d4735782d32888d730068a079c005e5286b4",
      "inventory_kind": "surrounding_prose_needs_semantic_classification",
      "review_status": "approved_complete_source",
      "semantic_kind": "proved",
      "material_status": "proved",
      "lean_declarations": [
        "SafeLearning.PrimersFoundations.square_not_injective",
        "SafeLearning.PrimersFoundations.square_not_surjective",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteFoundationsLogic.square_to_nonnegative_classification",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteFoundationsLogic.square_from_nonnegative_classification",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteFoundationsLogic.nonnegative_square_inverse",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteFoundationsFunctionInverseRange.actual_range_restricted_map_is_bijective_iff_original_is_injective"
      ],
      "hypotheses": [
        "The word can denotes possible removal, witnessed by the four declared square maps; restricting a target remains a well-defined function only if it contains the actual image."
      ],
      "missing_clauses": [],
      "per_unit_reason": "The same square formula has actual nonnegative-domain injectivity and actual nonnegative-target surjectivity while its real-real versions fail them. The range-restricted generic map is truly bijective iff original injective. No claim that every arbitrary restriction succeeds is inferred."
    },
    {
      "key": "primer-basics.html::node-549",
      "source": "SafeLearning/primer-basics.html",
      "locator": "::node-549",
      "line": 565,
      "tag": "p",
      "classes": [],
      "source_text": "Domain and codomain are part of a function\u2019s specification; its formula alone does not determine these properties.",
      "text_sha256": "9e08d05820cebcce9db7c381a2a876658e97767c722cfc078e6217efa0d2da5b",
      "source_sha256": "7aded044a9c433031999bcd57319d4735782d32888d730068a079c005e5286b4",
      "inventory_kind": "surrounding_prose_needs_semantic_classification",
      "review_status": "approved_complete_source",
      "semantic_kind": "proved",
      "material_status": "proved",
      "lean_declarations": [
        "SafeLearning.PrimersFoundations.square_not_injective",
        "SafeLearning.PrimersFoundations.square_not_surjective",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteFoundationsLogic.square_to_nonnegative_classification",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteFoundationsLogic.square_from_nonnegative_classification",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteFoundationsLogic.nonnegative_square_inverse"
      ],
      "hypotheses": [
        "The exact four domain/codomain choices in F5."
      ],
      "missing_clauses": [],
      "per_unit_reason": "The genuine same-formula maps have different proved injective/surjective properties. Thus the formula alone cannot determine these properties; the statement is directly witnessed by actual typed functions and not classified as an empty title."
    },
    {
      "key": "primer-basics.html::node-558",
      "source": "SafeLearning/primer-basics.html",
      "locator": "::node-558",
      "line": 580,
      "tag": "div",
      "classes": [],
      "source_text": "Subtract the fixed-point equation from the iteration. The sign alternates, but the absolute error halves.",
      "text_sha256": "b252feb8eb9047f7dd8f1b2d4f3a3f4d7cf56a58da721c1427bd4a51fc914a70",
      "source_sha256": "7aded044a9c433031999bcd57319d4735782d32888d730068a079c005e5286b4",
      "inventory_kind": "surrounding_prose_needs_semantic_classification",
      "review_status": "approved_complete_source",
      "semantic_kind": "proved",
      "material_status": "proved",
      "lean_declarations": [
        "SafeLearning.PrimersFoundations.alternate_fixed_point",
        "SafeLearning.PrimersFoundations.alternate_error",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteFoundationsLogic.affine_iterated_error",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteFoundationsLogic.alternating_iteration_error"
      ],
      "hypotheses": [
        "Actual map -x/2+3 with fixedpoint2 and initial0."
      ],
      "missing_clauses": [],
      "per_unit_reason": "The exact affine-error formula is obtained by subtracting the actual fixedpoint and genuine induction yields -2*(-1/2)^k. The actual absolute formula 2*(1/2)^k establishes alternation and halving for every index, with no positive-slope premise."
    },
    {
      "key": "primer-basics.html::node-573",
      "source": "SafeLearning/primer-basics.html",
      "locator": "::node-573",
      "line": 600,
